Flush stdout on its own first when flushing all streams.
winansi_fflush(NULL) flushed every stream at once and, only if that
reported a failure, used the last Windows error to decide whether
stdout was a broken pipe. Not every C runtime reports one: Wine's
fflush(NULL) returns 0 even when writing stdout failed, and when a
failure is reported the last error may belong to another stream. The
broken pipe was then missed. Under Wine, watch, which flushes with
fflush_all() on each cycle, kept running forever with its output going
nowhere, and a shell builtin or NOFORK applet writing to such a pipe
got status 1 instead of 141. Flush stdout first, with its own check,
then the other streams, and return failure if either failed.

---
 win32/winansi.c |   14 ++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/win32/winansi.c
+++ b/win32/winansi.c
@@ -952,12 +952,18 @@
 
 int FAST_FUNC winansi_fflush(FILE *stream)
 {
-	int ret;
+	int ret = 0;
 
-	/* fflush(NULL) doesn't say which stream failed: assume stdout */
+	/* fflush(NULL) doesn't say which stream failed, and may not report
+	 * a failure at all: flush stdout on its own first */
+	if (!stream)
+		ret = winansi_fflush(stdout);
 	SetLastError(0);
-	if ((ret=fflush(stream)) == EOF)
-		check_pipe(stream ? stream : stdout);
+	if (fflush(stream) == EOF) {
+		if (stream)
+			check_pipe(stream);
+		ret = EOF;
+	}
 	return ret;
 }
 
